  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "Full published paper text is present.",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "Compares test performance across architectures and training recipes on natural CT labels, but does not test any mapped version's specified setup (such as random-label memorization or convolutional versus fully connected networks).",
    "gate_note": "The study reports training, validation and test accuracy and other test metrics for five architectures and 20 architecture/loss/optimizer experiments. These are results about architecture and generalization on natural labels, but do not directly measure the particular behaviors named in V1–V8. No random-label training, width sweep, fully connected comparison, SGD comparison, batch-size sweep, dropout intervention, sharpness measure, or removal of memorized examples is reported.",
    "test_reason": "The study compares pretrained CNNs and a transformer after short fine-tuning on natural CT labels. None of its results tests V1–V8. Section 2.3 cites optimizer findings relevant to V4 to justify choosing Adam and AdamW, but includes no SGD comparator or demonstrated generalization deficit for decoupled weight decay to recover.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is accurate. The authors conclude that ResNet-50 architectures perform robustly on this three-class CT task, with ResNet-50r optimized using AdamW ranked best overall. They suggest that less resource-demanding architectures can outperform more complex ones and explicitly disclaim causal inference about why the architectures differ.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses transfer learning from ImageNet and fine-tunes on natural medical CT labels for eight epochs with Adam or AdamW; it does not train from scratch, use random labels, or test the mapped interventions.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Architecture, loss function and optimizer affected reported rankings and metrics. ResNet-50r with AdamW ranked highly; ResNet-50r with wCE and Adam was a low-performing configuration. The study held batch size, learning rate and epochs fixed.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Architecture-level test accuracy ranges from 98.55% for CaiT to 99.02% for ResNet-50. ResNet-50r with AdamW achieves 99.16% with CE and 99.13% with weighted CE, and receives the best experiment rankings overall.",
        "proposed_claim": "Pretrained image architectures and optimizer/loss combinations differ in CT classification performance after eight epochs of fine-tuning.",
